Subject: dxgi/winex11: scope DComp popup inference by thread and process

The popup transient anchor and the full-versus-popup mode choice used
one process-global stack and count: a second plugin's first target
became a popup of the first plugin (losing its present timer), and its
popups could anchor to the wrong plugin's window. Both now key on the
target's UI thread; a plugin creates its popups on its own thread, so
thread affinity relates a popup to its plugin.

The desktop-wide __wine_dcomp_active flag becomes per-process
(__wine_dcomp_active_<pid>): the desktop property list spans the window
station, so the session-wide flag gave unrelated processes' ownerless
tool popups a menu role while any DComp GUI existed anywhere, the
undismissable-dialog class of faults. In-process behaviour is
unchanged.

The thread keying rests on two more things, so this patch fixes both.
Every plugin pushes and removes on its own UI thread, so a lock now
moves the stack, its new thread-id array and the depth together. Without
one, two plugins opening editors at the same moment tear the arrays
apart and a popup anchors to an unrelated plugin's window.

A repeat SET_TARGET also stops subclassing a target twice.
SetWindowLongPtrW would hand back our own proc as the original, our proc
would then forward to itself on every message, and teardown would
install it as the window's own wndproc with the application's proc lost.
That second install also inflated the subclassed count and the popup
stack, which teardown unwinds only once.

WINE_DCOMP_SCOPE=global restores the process-global stack, count and
session-wide flag for comparison.

diff --git a/dlls/dxgi/factory.c b/dlls/dxgi/factory.c
index 9134a50..e338edd 100644
--- a/dlls/dxgi/factory.c
+++ b/dlls/dxgi/factory.c
@@ -580,13 +580,39 @@ LONG dcomp_subclassed_target_count;
